// A spiral stair, and not one line of it says where anything goes.
//
// Every tread, every baluster and every rail knob in this picture is placed by asking one of four
// functions.  There are sixty-six things standing here and four numbers that decide them all: how far
// round each step turns, how far it rises, how far out it reaches, and how high a rail stands above
// the tread it guards.  Change `perStep` from 24 to 18 and the whole stair becomes a gentler helix,
// treads and balusters and knobs together, because none of them was ever told a position -- they were
// told a rule.
//
// That is the whole point of a scene being able to write functions.  Before, this stair would have
// been sixty-six hand-computed transforms, and making it climb a little faster would have meant
// editing sixty-six of them and getting one wrong.
//
// Look at what the four functions are doing, because between them they cover most of what a function
// is for:
//
//   turnOf   is plain arithmetic on the step number.
//   riseOf   is the same, and is used by two other things besides the treads.
//   reachOf  works something out on the way -- the growth so far -- and then uses it.  That is what a
//            body's workings are for: naming a piece of the sum so the reader can see what it means.
//   railAt   calls riseOf.  A function may lean on another, so the rail follows the stair without
//            anybody having to keep two rules in step by hand.
//
// The last of those is worth dwelling on.  The rail is not at "a height that happens to match the
// treads"; it is at *the tread's height plus a bit*, said once.  Alter the rise and the rail comes
// along, because it was never a separate fact.
//
// Every one of them has a fallback, so a call says only what it is changing.  `reachOf(step)` is the
// common case and reads as one thing; `reachOf(step, 1.4)` is the same stair, wider.  That division --
// a rich declaration and a short call -- is deliberate: the person writing the function and the person
// writing the scene are usually not the same person, and the second one should not have to know how
// the first one works.
//
// The light is the physical sky, which needs saying only where the sun stands; see the sky scene in
// the lights directory for what that does.

context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    angles are degrees
    info {
        title 'A Spiral Stair'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'Sixty-six pieces placed by four functions, none of them ever told a position.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

// How far round the stair has turned by a given step.
function turnOf(step, perStep = 24) -> number {
    return step * perStep
}

// And how far it has climbed.
function riseOf(step, perStep = 0.30) -> number {
    return step * perStep
}

// How far out the middle of a tread sits.  The stair opens as it climbs, so this grows -- and the
// growing is named rather than buried in the sum, which is what workings are for.
function reachOf(step, inner = 1.05, flare = 0.035) -> number {
    grown = step * flare
    return inner + grown
}

// A rail stands a fixed height above the tread it guards, wherever that tread happens to be.  It
// leans on riseOf rather than repeating it, so the two can never fall out of step.
function railAt(step, above = 0.92) -> number {
    return riseOf(step) + above
}

camera {
    location [8.0, 3.2, -11.0]
    look at [0, 3.6, 0]
    field of view 46
}

background physical sky {
    sun elevation 34
    sun azimuth 128
    turbidity 2.4
    brightness 3
}

sky light {
    samples 40
}

plane {
    material {
        pigment [0.28, 0.27, 0.25]
        specular 0.08
    }
}

// The newel the whole stair turns about.
cylinder {
    min Y 0  max Y 7.2
    material { pigment [0.38, 0.27, 0.18]  specular 0.3  shininess 80 }
    scale [0.34, 1, 0.34]
}

// The treads.
group {
    step = [0, 21]
    cube {
        material { pigment [0.60, 0.44, 0.28]  specular 0.25  shininess 60 }
        scale [0.95, 0.06, 0.30]
        translate X reachOf(step)
        rotate Y turnOf(step)
        translate Y riseOf(step)
    }
}

// The balusters, each standing from its own tread up to its own rail.  Their height is the difference
// between two of the functions, which is why nothing here repeats a number.
group {
    step = [0, 21]
    cylinder {
        min Y 0  max Y 0.92
        material { pigment [0.70, 0.68, 0.62]  specular 0.45  shininess 140 }
        scale [0.028, 1, 0.028]
        translate X reachOf(step) + 0.78
        rotate Y turnOf(step)
        translate Y riseOf(step)
    }
}

// And the knob each baluster carries.
group {
    step = [0, 21]
    sphere {
        material { pigment [0.74, 0.72, 0.66]  specular 0.55  shininess 160 }
        scale 0.085
        translate X reachOf(step) + 0.78
        rotate Y turnOf(step)
        translate Y railAt(step)
    }
}
